---
/*
  StarBloom — the signature mark, blooming.

  A constellation is drawn BETWEEN stars, never one star, so a single ✦ beside
  a constellation's name is a small lie. Rather than redraw the sky's whole
  vocabulary to fix it, the mark simply parts when you attend to it: the core
  recedes and companions arrive from beyond, the way a point of light resolves
  into more than one under magnification. It says "several things live here"
  and then gets out of the way.

  The companions carry no data — not the fragment count, not weight. Weight is
  already said twice on the overview (type scale and glow) and a third telling
  would be clutter for a number nobody needs. This is a gesture, not a chart.

  Everything is sized in `em`, so one component serves a 5xl overview name and
  a 1rem floating control with no props. Only the core sits in normal flow —
  companions are absolutely positioned — so blooming NEVER reflows the text
  beside it.

  Blooms on `.group:hover`, so hovering anywhere on the constellation's row or
  link triggers it, not just the mark itself. Every call site therefore needs
  `group` on whichever ancestor should be the hover target. Styles live in
  app.css ("The bloom").

  TWO STATES, because the bloom is not only a hover flourish:

    · at rest   — a single point. You are outside; the constellation is
                  unresolved at distance, and hovering is a preview that
                  releases when you leave.
    · `active`  — held open. You are INSIDE this constellation, and it stays
                  parted for the whole visit rather than for the length of an
                  animation. Going back to the sky collapses it to a point
                  again, which the shared `transition:name` on the star
                  animates for free across the navigation.

  Hover always previews what the click will do, so on an active mark the
  gesture runs BACKWARDS: pointing at it (or at the title beside it) sends the
  companions back out and restores the single point — the close, rehearsed.

  Only the suite's header mark is active: it IS the constellation you are
  standing in. The ways back out (the outro, the floating ✦) stay at rest —
  they point at the sky, not at where you are.

  Decorative throughout: each mark is aria-hidden, and the enclosing link
  carries the accessible name.
*/
import StarMark from './StarMark.astro';

interface Props {
  class?: string;
  /** Held open — the constellation currently being read. */
  active?: boolean;
}
const { class: className = '', active = false } = Astro.props;

/* Four companions, deliberately uneven — the mark itself has an uneven reach
   (star-mark.ts), and a symmetrical ring of them would read as machinery.
   Offsets are the prototype's, converted to `em` so they hold at any size. */
const COMPANIONS = [
  { dx: '-1.00em', dy: '-0.69em' },
  { dx: '1.07em', dy: '-0.48em' },
  { dx: '-0.65em', dy: '0.97em' },
  { dx: '0.95em', dy: '0.84em' },
];
---

<span class:list={['star-bloom', active && 'is-active', className]}>
  <span class="star-bloom__core"><StarMark /></span>
  {
    COMPANIONS.map((c) => (
      <span class="star-bloom__sat" style={`--dx:${c.dx}; --dy:${c.dy}`}>
        <StarMark />
      </span>
    ))
  }
</span>
